     Typeset`initDone$$ = True),
    SynchronousInitialization->True,
    UndoTrackedVariables:>{Typeset`show$$, Typeset`bookmarkMode$$},
    UnsavedVariables:>{Typeset`initDone$$},
    UntrackedVariables:>{Typeset`size$$}], "Manipulate",
   Deployed->True,
   StripOnInput->False],
  Manipulate`InterpretManipulate[1]]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[1]=",ExpressionUUID->"69126ee1-1dc9-452e-8dc3-ffee8d20fc29"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Documentation", "Section",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"e5b48c76-9ef9-435c-93ae-347850a12596"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook shows the phase portrait of the two-population replicator \
dynamics in any 2-player 2-action repeated game with endogenous separation. \
There is one population for each role in the game. Partners are drawn from \
different populations, play the stage game repeatedly with each other, and \
either of them may leave the partnership at any time.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8c845bcb-a751-4dec-8482-ce147cb1ad0b"],

Cell["\<\
Each subpopulation contains two strategies, so the state of the system is a \
pair of numbers, the fraction of the first strategy in each subpopulation, \
and the state space is the unit square. Individuals whose partnership has \
ended return to a pool of singles and are rematched at random with a single \
from the other subpopulation. Because strategies that end their partnerships \
sooner are rematched more often, the composition of the pool generally \
differs from the composition of the population; the program computes the pool \
that is consistent with each population state, and evaluates payoffs there.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1a4b8d55-7c18-4c9a-9a5d-2e3a7f61a001"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parameters", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"590dddd8-31a9-4c5f-b4c8-2bf175fb5cbb"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Payoffs", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"c076fe37-74af-4c6c-9752-2049e3ce6a44"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The stage game is defined by two payoff matrices, one for each \
subpopulation, whose entries are written ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CapitalPi]", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b39d4a77-9bb4-40af-9831-983ee13d39e3"],
 ", the payoff obtained by a player of that subpopulation who takes action i \
against a partner who takes action j. The two actions are C (cooperate) and D \
(defect). The two matrices may differ, so the stage game need not be \
symmetric. After changing the payoffs, the user should click on the button \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Update\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] to run the computations \
with the new payoffs. The program also contains a set of predefined games \
which can be inspected by clicking on the chooser with the label \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Predefined games\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. \
Subpopulation 1 is the one drawn on the horizontal axis of the figure, and \
subpopulation 2 the one on the vertical axis.\
"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7d6bdaef-365b-4320-ac7c-966c6db12e1c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Strategies", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"2eaed39b-5511-4ec6-91fc-673e4fd1cbe8"],

Cell["Strategies are determined by the following five \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]genes\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->\
"43be6ad7-59cb-434b-aebd-390f8849047f"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Initial: The first gene determines the individual\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s first action, i.e. the action (C or D) when the \
individual interacts with her partner for the very first time.", "Item"]], \
"Item",ExpressionUUID->"81895113-ea99-4768-8374-34d1c25411bc"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["After CC: The second gene determines what to do when \
the outcome of the interaction has been CC, i.e. both players have played C. \
The options are:", "Item"]], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"6816733d-a258-49e1-93e7-\
234ff6e5e299"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["C: Stay with the current partner and, assuming the \
partnership continues, play C in the following time-step.", "Item"]], \
"Subitem",ExpressionUUID->"52dc2971-0320-4945-ad8e-b742af5bf4ca"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["D: Stay with the current partner and, assuming the \
partnership continues, play D in the following time-step.", "Item"]], \
"Subitem",ExpressionUUID->"98ed99d6-b4eb-4137-970f-51c7a4573dfe"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["L: Leave the current partnership. In this case, the \
partnership is broken regardless of the decision of the partner.", "Item"]], \
"Subitem",ExpressionUUID->"8dcab4ef-85dc-4142-9d80-a1cf4986801b"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["After CD: The third gene determines the choice -also \
C, D or L- after outcome CD, which denotes the situation where the individual \
has played C and the partner has played D.", "Item"]], "Item",ExpressionUUID->\
"8f300e86-53bd-4204-870e-3fc7abb719d3"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["After DC: The fourth gene determines the choice (C, D \
or L) after outcome DC, which denotes the situation where the individual has \
played D and the partner has played C.", "Item"]], "Item",ExpressionUUID->\
"ca3b8f61-65f3-4408-aaa0-0b2432880027"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["After DD: Finally, the fifth gene determines the \
choice (C, D or L) after outcome DD, i.e. when the two players have defected. \
", "Item"]], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"9ed479f3-6a45-4384-a848-ba0ebda4f1af"],

Cell["\<\
A strategy thus specifies a course of action at every possible juncture in an \
individual\[CloseCurlyQuote]s life. We permit the 2\[CenterDot]3\[CenterDot]3\
\[CenterDot]3\[CenterDot]3 = 162 possible strategies. Each subpopulation \
contains two of them, which gives (162\[CenterDot]161)/2 = 13041 possible \
pairs per subpopulation, and 13041\[CenterDot]13041 = 170067681 \
configurations of the two-population model. The two subpopulations may use \
different pairs of strategies, and a subpopulation may also be given the same \
strategy twice.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6d9bfa5c-7bc9-47bc-ae71-759b6ce57154"],

Cell["\<\
Strategies that have a name are shown by that name; the rest are shown by \
their five genes, written as a string of Cs, Ds and Ls. The chooser at the \
right of each row offers a selection of named strategies.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3b8f4d21-5c10-4ee1-9d4a-0fbc5a3c1102"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Probability of continuation \[Delta]", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"c31057d9-c246-4446-9322-91110e3310ce"],

Cell["\<\
A partnership can end in two ways. It ends endogenously, when at least one of \
the partners plays L, which is determined by the two strategies alone. It \
also ends exogenously: after every stage, and independently of everything \
else, the partnership survives with probability \[Delta] and dissolves with \
probability 1 - \[Delta]. If the two strategies of a pair prescribe playing \
together for T stages before someone leaves, the expected duration of the \
partnership is (1 - \[Delta]^T)/(1 - \[Delta]), which is read as 1/(1 - \
\[Delta]) when the pair never separates endogenously. These expected \
durations are what makes the pool of singles differ from the population.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4b87dbdd-2993-4142-a774-6fd50bd07639"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Probability of mutation", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"bfaf77e0-e6d2-4970-b577-864e9e9975b4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The basic dynamic ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    OverscriptBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "k"], "."], "=",
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["f", "k"], "(",
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["x", "1"], ",",
      SubscriptBox["x", "2"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a12ed43a-8112-413f-a380-42ea86878076"],
 " can be modified by allowing revising players to choose their new strategy \
at random (among the 2 allowed in their own subpopulation) with a certain \
probability m. Formally, the altered dynamic which includes this feature \
reads as follows: "
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7a73b3c6-6ad5-40f9-b61d-cc385c114f05"],

Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   OverscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["x", "k"], "."], "=",
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(",
      RowBox[{"1", "-", "m"}], ")"}], " ",
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["f", "k"], "(",
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["x", "1"], ",",
       SubscriptBox["x", "2"]}], ")"}]}], "+",
    RowBox[{"m", " ",
     RowBox[{"(",
      RowBox[{
       FractionBox["1", "2"], "-",
       SubscriptBox["x", "k"]}], ")"}]}]}]}],
  TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->"2714c35c-4ed8-4281-8207-ab8caa5ce4d0"]], \
"Text",ExpressionUUID->"dc6836c9-62ff-4dcf-ba8e-ccd844a6dd73"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here k is 1 or 2, and ",
 
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["x", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d7022ba4-888a-4bb5-bef3-42ca8565c399"],
 " is the fraction of the first strategy in subpopulation k."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c0e3f5a2-9d7b-4a8e-8b12-6f9a0c4b7e21"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Plot", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"bed87209-639e-4e3a-80bb-89dca27c294f"],

Cell["\<\
The setter labeled \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]type\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
chooses how the dynamics are drawn: StreamPlot, the default, draws \
streamlines, and VectorPlot draws arrows on a grid. The slider labeled \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]vector size\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] scales the arrows \
of the vector plot, and has no effect on the stream plot. The setter labeled \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]background?\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] controls whether \
the background is colored according to the speed of motion.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"26f7cd6e-d2ee-40d9-bc15-744401e7e367"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Only black & white", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"0448ceb8-bcc3-46b5-9eab-a0988a41dad9"],

Cell["The setter labeled \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Only black & white\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] redraws the figure without any color, for a document that is to be printed in black and white. The background becomes a gray scale, whatever gradient is chosen below, and it stops short of black so that the streamlines and the arrows stay visible over it. The streamlines and the arrows are black whether or not the background is drawn. The rest points take the gray tones listed under Output, and the curves that mark the components of rest points and the titles of the two tables under the figure, which are red otherwise, are drawn in black. The exported pdf follows the setting, since what is exported is the figure on screen.", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0405ac78-f880-40b0-b5d0-fb4ae91ec966"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Color gradient", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"16532bda-d780-4b6b-a32c-2ddb182260e0"],

Cell["\<\
The user can choose among 52 different color gradients to use for the \
(background) density plot showing the speed of the dynamic. The two arrows at \
the sides of the row page through them.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"72322232-5053-4331-bd79-d609f3dfb477"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Maximum time out", "Subsubsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"5d2a1b77-4c6e-4a1f-93f6-8b1c2d0f4e77"],

Cell["\<\
The rest points are computed exactly, by symbolic methods, and for some \
combinations of parameters this can take a long time. maxTimeOut is the \
number of seconds allowed for that computation, for the drawing of any \
components of rest points, and for their classification. If the limit is \
exceeded, the phase portrait is still drawn and a message under it says so.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2f0c9a31-4d8b-4f2e-9a03-5b1d7c8e4a66"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Output", "Subsection",
 CellGroupingRules->{
  "SectionGrouping",
   Inherited},ExpressionUUID->"597b3f87-ed10-4d79-b4a4-749e420985f7"],

Cell["\<\
The program shows a figure that includes the phase portrait of the chosen \
dynamics on the unit square. The horizontal axis is subpopulation 1 and the \
vertical axis is subpopulation 2; each axis runs from all of that \
subpopulation\[CloseCurlyQuote]s second strategy, at the low end, to all of \
its first strategy, at the high end, and is labeled with the two strategy \
names.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6b7ccf26-cf8a-48fc-aa7a-33705c52788c"],

Cell["\<\
Isolated rest points are drawn as discs, colored by the eigenvalues of the \
Jacobian of the dynamics at the point: green for a sink, where both \
eigenvalues have negative real part; red for a source, where both are \
positive; white for a saddle, where the real parts have opposite signs; \
purple for a linear center, where the eigenvalues are purely imaginary; and \
gray when the linearization leaves the type undetermined, which happens when \
an eigenvalue vanishes. A legend under the portrait lists the types that \
occur. The Jacobian is obtained by implicit differentiation of the conditions \
that relate the pool with the population, so it is exact and the \
classification does not rest on any numerical tolerance. With the setter \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Only black & white\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] on, the same \
five types are drawn in gray tones instead: black for a sink, white for a \
source, mid gray for a saddle, light gray for a linear center, and dark gray \
when the type is undetermined.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8a1f2b64-3d07-4e51-9c1a-7b2e0f5d3c48"],

Cell["\<\
A linear center need not be a center of the nonlinear dynamics. The color \
describes the linearization only, and by itself establishes neither stability \
nor the existence of periodic orbits.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4c7d9e10-2b83-4f6a-9d51-8e0a1c7b2f39"],

Cell["\<\
When the rest points are not isolated but form components, these are drawn as \
red curves. They are not classified, since along a component the \
linearization necessarily has a zero eigenvalue.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9e5b3a27-6c14-4d82-a7f3-1b6c5d0e9a48"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Under the figure, the exact coordinates of the isolated rest points and the \
exact description of the components are also shown as tables. In them, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubsuperscriptBox["x", "i", 
    RowBox[{"(", "k", ")"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5becc54e-fb40-4b49-b4c0-5f3e29d15863"],
 " is the fraction of strategy i in subpopulation k, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubsuperscriptBox["p", "i", 
    RowBox[{"(", "k", ")"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d4a8dce7-6681-4ba7-a37b-db69dc4984f7"],
 " is the same fraction in its pool of singles, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubsuperscriptBox["F", "i", 
    RowBox[{"(", "k", ")"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "06457537-ad2c-4f05-9f80-f2fb76912acd"],
 " is the payoff to strategy i in subpopulation k: the superscript is the \
subpopulation and the subscript the strategy. Both strategies of each \
subpopulation are listed, in the population and in the pool, even though \
their shares add up to one, so that each distribution can be read off \
directly. The table of isolated rest points also gives the two eigenvalues ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "1"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "64cb1917-3912-4cb0-be41-ada1774584f6"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Lambda]", "2"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "140cee5b-d55f-41eb-aa1e-d7c0cd4978ee"],
 " of the Jacobian at each point, so the color code can be checked against \
the numbers."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7d1c6e35-4a92-4b18-8c63-5f2a9b0d1e47"],

Cell["\<\
The displayed figure can be exported in vector pdf format by clicking on \
the button \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Export figure\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. The \
production of this file (the .pdf figure) is not permitted in the free \
Wolfram Player. It requires Mathematica. The button \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Generate full report\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] opens a \
new notebook containing the parameters, the figure, and the exact rest points \
together with the exact eigenvalues at them.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5b8e2f41-3c7d-4a96-b25e-0c9d6a3f8b12"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

